    "name":  "Delirium",
    "module":  "geriatric",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Delirium is an acute neuropsychiatric syndrome characterised by disturbances in attention, awareness, and cognition that develop over a short period and fluctuate in severity. It affects up to 30% of hospitalised elderly patients and is associated with increased mortality, prolonged hospitalisation, and long-term cognitive decline. Three subtypes exist: hyperactive (agitation, combativeness), hypoactive (the most common and most missed — drowsiness, withdrawal), and mixed. The Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) is the standard diagnostic tool. Common precipitants include infections (UTI, pneumonia), metabolic disturbances, medications (anticholinergics, opioids, benzodiazepines), pain, constipation, urinary retention, and sensory deprivation. Treatment is non-pharmacological first; antipsychotics are used sparingly.",
